e percent all point the same way. The two Democratic-sponsored polls showing a four-point race are too few and too skewed to outweigh that convergence.", "watch": "Additional independent polling or a further shift in the national environment could narrow the expected margin, though Davis would need several points of movement to put the seat in play.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Bill Cassidy (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Louisiana's Senate race is an open-seat contest between Republican Julia Letlow, a sitting congresswoman, and Democrat Jamie Davis, after Bill Cassidy failed to secure renomination. The state's Republican lean of about eleven points forms the backdrop, though the national political environment appears somewhat more favorable to Democrats than in recent cycles. Letlow holds a clear financial edge, raising roughly $6.2 million to Davis's $2.0 million, and outside groups have poured more than $11 million into the race on each side.\n\nOnly two public polls exist, both commissioned by Democratic-leaning sponsors, and they show Letlow ahead by four points, closer than the state's usual margins. Ten expert ratings all favor Republicans, most calling the race solid or safe, and prediction markets put Davis's chances between four and nine percent. The forecast expects Letlow to win by a high-single-digit margin, with Davis needing a substantially better environment than current evidence suggests.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0491, "margin": -9.91}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0664, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0312, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0328, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0575, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0895, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0752, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0962, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1593, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2